<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">J Biophys Biochem Cytol</journal-id><journal-title>The Journal of Biophysical and Biochemical Cytology</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0095-9901</issn><publisher><publisher-name>The Rockefeller University Press</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">13758938</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2225045</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>FINE STRUCTURE OF THE CILIA OF ROTIFERS</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Lansing</surname><given-names>Albert I.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Lamy</surname><given-names>Francois</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="N0x1ccfa80N0x34777f8">From the Department of Anatomy, School of Medicine, University of Pittsburgh, and the Marine Biological Laboratory, Woods Hole</aff><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><day>1</day><month>4</month><year>1961</year></pub-date><volume>9</volume><issue>4</issue><fpage>799</fpage><lpage>812</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>7</day><month>12</month><year>1960</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; Copyright, 1961, by The Rockefeller Institute Press</copyright-statement></permissions><abstract><p> The fine structure of the coronal cilia of the rotifer <italic>Philodina citrina</italic> has been studied in detail. Specimens were fixed with OsO<sub>4</sub> and embedded in butyl&#x02014;methyl methacrylate, Epon 812, or Vestopal and sectioned with a Porter-Blum microtome. The details of structure of the rootlets, basal bodies, basal plates, and free cilia are described. The general structure of the rotifer ciliary apparatus conforms well to that established for other species. One of the main observations is the difference in structure of the peripheral filaments in the opposing halves of a cross-section of the free cilium. Also, in longitudinal sections evidence is offered for the existence of a helical structure in the peripheral filaments. </p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>